# DESCRIPTION

Loinnir is the SDK's GPU library, in the style that has no graphics API of its own: one device and one queue, one bindless heap of textures, one pipeline layout (the heap and a root pointer for each draw), memory addressed by its GPU address, and one 64-bit timeline that every submission counts up.
It is written on the core (gpucore(2)) and on nothing under it, and a backend of the core does the work: the one Loinnir opens is chosen by what the GPU class offers.

A GPU whose driver host offers the `csf` capset gets the native Mali driver, which draws and computes on the GPU's own command streams; its sampled textures, sRGB targets and alpha blending are the core's (gpucore(2)).
Any other gets Vulkan: our loader with the Venus client driver linked in, whose commands go through the GPU class to the display driver's `gpu` service (virtio-gpu(3)), and the host's renderer runs them on the host's GPU.
A program reaches the GPU through `/dev`, which it mounts from devmgr (`mount /dev devmgr/dev`); without one, a call fails with `.vulkan`.
`gpu.gpu.name` and `gpu.gpu.backend` say which one it is.
On the dev machine, Loinnir uses the host's Vulkan, or ours over Venus and vtest when `TODHCHAI_VULKAN_LIBRARY` names `libtdvulkan.so`.

`headless` opens the first GPU with no window, for offscreen work.
`open(window:loop:)` opens one for a window of sdk(2)'s loop, whose frames are a ring of three images of the core (gpucore(2)'s present images) that the window is given as its own with the SDK's `addImage`.
`frame` gives a texture of the window's size to draw into: one of the ring that Radharc reads no more, or nil if it reads all three (the frame is dropped; a change of size makes the ring again).
`present` presents it once the GPU has reached the value `submit` returned: where the device presents direct (the Mali backend), the image is the window's own and nothing is copied; where it can't share the image's own pages with another process (under Venus), the GPU copies the image into the memory the window shows, in a list of its own; the CPU copies and reads no pixel either way. Where the device's timeline is a counter it gives (`timelineHandle`, the Mali backend's), the window takes it as each image's acquire counter and `present` names the value, so it does not wait; elsewhere it waits on the CPU for the GPU's work before it presents.
A present the window has no credit for (its last present has not been shown yet) is dropped, and `presentCounts` says how many frames were presented and how many dropped.
Where the device can share neither images nor memory, or can't share a frame of the window's size, `frame` throws `.unsupported`.

`alloc` gives memory of a kind: `.device` for the GPU alone, `.upload` that the CPU writes, `.readback` that the CPU reads; its `address` is what a draw's root pointer names, and its `pointer` the CPU's.
`texture` makes a texture in the heap; its `index` is how a shader names it.
With `sampled: false` it makes one no shader reads (a target to draw into and copy out of), which needs no heap slot of the driver's: `canSample` says whether the driver can sample textures at all, and where it can't, `texture` with `sampled: true` throws `.unsupported`.
`texture` with `scanout: true` (a render target that is not sampled) makes one a display shows as it is, and `exportVMO(_ texture:)` gives a new handle to its VMO, for the display to attach (linlon(3)); nil where the driver can't share it so.
`importTexture` makes a texture over another process's image (a window's frame, in a VMO: `width` by `height` four-byte pixels in rows of `width`), sampled where it lies and never copied (on the Mali backend, where the rows are a multiple of 64 bytes); the caller keeps the VMO, and it is `.unsupported` where the device can't import (gpucore(2)'s `importHandle`, the Mali backend's).
`pipeline` takes SPIR-V for a vertex and a fragment shader.
A `CommandList` records `render(to:clear:)` with its draws (`draw(_:root:vertices:)`, `scissor`), and `copy` between textures and memory; `submit` returns the timeline's value at which it is done, which `wait` waits for.

